es>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Chejfec</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Use of colonoscopy to screen asymptomatic adults for colorectal cancer</article-title><source>N Engl J Med</source><year>2000</year><volume>343</volume><fpage>162</fpage><lpage>168</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10900274</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B7"><label>7</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Dominitz</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><name><surname>Provenzale</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Patient preferences and quality of life associated with colorectal cancer screening</article-title><source>Am J Gastroenterol</source><year>1997</year><volume>92</volume><fpage>2171</fpage><lpage>2178</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9399747</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B8"><label>8</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Vining</surname><given-names>DJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Gelfand</surname><given-names>DW</given-names></name><name><surname>Bechtold</surname><given-names>RE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Technical feasibility of colon imaging with helical CT and virtual reality</article-title><source>AJR Am J Roentgenol</source><year>1994</year><volume>162</volume><issue>Suppl</issue><fpage>104</fpage></citation></ref><ref id="B9"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Angtuaco</surname><given-names>TL</given-names></name><name><surname>Banaad-Omiotek</surname><given-names>GD</given-names></name><name><surname>Howden</surname><given-names>CW</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Differing attitudes toward virtual and conventional colonoscopy for colorectal cancer screening: surveys among primary care physicians and potential patients</article-title><source>Am J G